### 4.4 Jet Energy Scale Uncertainties

The uncertainties on the trigger jet energy scale are assessed under two extreme and opposite assumptions.

1. _HT trigger jets in Au+Au are unmodified compared to HT trigger jets in_ p+p. In this scenario, the shape of the jet spectrum in Au+Au should match the shape of the jet spectrum of _p+p_ jets embedded in a heavy ion background. As shown in Fig. 4.4, the shapes of the spectra do not match even after detector effects are accounted for. A shift of \(\Delta E=+1\) GeV/\(c\) in the _p+p_ trigger jet

Figure 4.4: (a) Detector-level \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,rec}\) spectra of HT trigger jets in _p+p_ and Au+Au, and of _p+p_ HT trigger jets embedded in Au+Au MB events. (b) Ratio of \((1/N)dN/dp_{\rm T}^{Au+Au}\) to \((1/N)dN/dp_{\rm T}^{p+p,{\rm emb}}\) with uncertainties due to the relative tracking efficiency, relative tower energy, and \(\Delta E\) shift. The ratio of \((1/N)dN/dp_{\rm T}^{Au+Au}\) to \((1/N)dN/dp_{\rm T}^{p+p}\) is also shown.

energy encompasses the difference in the shapes, as demonstrated by the \(\Delta E\) systematic uncertainty band in Fig. 4.4(b) which encompasses unity for most values of \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,rec}\). As an explicit example, the \(\Delta E\) shift is applied in the \(10<p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,rec}<15\) GeV/\(c\) bin by determining the Au+Au jet energy distribution (the \(p_{\rm T}^{p\,+p,\rm emb}\) points in Fig. 4.3) for the range \(11<p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,rec,\,\it p+p}<16\) GeV/\(c\), and recalculating the correlation functions for that distribution of \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,Au+Au}\).
2. _HT trigger jets in Au+Au are maximally modified compared to HT trigger jets in_ p+p. In this case, jets originating with similar parton energies would not have equivalent reconstructed jet energies, because \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) is calculated only from fragments with \(p_{\rm T}>2\) GeV/\(c\). However, if all the missing fragments at high-\(p_{\rm T}\) reappear as low-\(p_{\rm T}\) particles, then the energy of the jets in Au+Au and _p+p_ should balance exactly (in other words, \(\Sigma D_{AA}=0\) on the nearside) when the parton energies are matched correctly. Therefore another systematic uncertainty is defined by the shift in the _p+p_ trigger jet energy necessary to force NS \(\Sigma D_{AA}\) to zero, under the assumption that the background is flat (as explained in Section 4.6). The \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,\,\it p+p}\) range is shifted downwards in steps of 1 GeV/\(c\) until the NS \(\Sigma D_{AA}\) (when calculated under the assumption that \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}=v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}=0\)) falls below zero. Table 4.1 shows the magnitudes of NS \(\Sigma D_{AA}\) before and after the shifts, as well as the necessary shift to the jet energy. Two observations should be noted from Table 4.1. First, in the lowest jet \(p_{\rm T}\) range, shifts of \(-3\) GeV/\(c\) or more start interfering with the HT threshold of 6 GeV and producing strange behaviors in the jet energy distributions. This is why the \(\Sigma D_{AA}\) does not quite get below zero. Second, in the middle jet bin, fitting issues in the lowest \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) bin cause the NS \(\Sigma D_{AA}\) to be abnormally large (this is also apparent in Fig. 4.10). Hence the shift to restore \(\Sigma D_{AA}\) to zero is also large, and is probably an overestimate.

### 4.5 Correlation Functions

Once a HT trigger jet has been found in an event, the associated hadron correlation functions are constructed with respect to the angle of the jet axis. The jet-hadron correlation functions are distributions of \(\Delta\phi=\phi_{\rm assoc}-\phi_{\rm jet}\), where \(\phi_{\rm jet}\) denotes the azimuthal angle of the axis of the reconstructed trigger jet and the associated particles are all charged hadrons in the event. The \(\Delta\phi\) correlations are analyzed in bins in the reconstructed transverse momentum of the trigger jet (\(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,rec}\)) and in the transverse momentum of the associated hadrons (\(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\)).

#### Acceptance & Efficiency Corrections

It is necessary to correct the \(\Delta\phi\) distributions for the TPC acceptance and efficiency. The probability of accurately reconstructing a charged track in the TPC depends on \(\phi\), \(\eta\), and \(p_{\rm T}\), and is accounted for by single- and two-particle efficiency corrections. The single particle tracking efficiency is an absolute correction, which adjusts the particle yields as a function of \(\eta\) and \(p_{\rm T}\), while the pair efficiency is a relative correction, adjusting the shapes of the \(\Delta\phi\) distributions.

The methods for determining the single particle tracking efficiency in both _p+p_ and Au+Au environments are discussed in Appendix A, and the efficiencies in _p+p_ and Au+Au are shown as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) in Fig. 4.5. When the \(\Delta\phi\) histograms are created, each track is weighted by the inverse of the single particle tracking efficiency.

The pair efficiency correction accounts for the \(\phi\)-dependence of the detector inefficiencies, and also depends on the track \(p_{\rm T}\). This correction is done by "event mixing:" a \(\Delta\phi|_{\rm mix}=\phi_{\rm assoc}^{\rm event2}-\phi_{\rm jet}^{\rm event1}\) histogram is created in which the trigger jet is from one event and the associated particles are from another event. Since the trigger and associates are from different events and therefore uncorrelated, any structure to the \(\Delta\phi|_{\rm mix}\) correlations is due to detector effects instead of physics. In this analysis, each trigger jet in Au+Au is mixed with the charged tracks from 50 other 0-20% central Au+Au HT events, and each trigger jet in _p+p_ is mixed with 50 other _p+p_ HT events. The mixing is not done in \(v_{z}\) bins or fine centrality bins because of limited statistics. The mixed event distributions are normalized so that the highest bin is at unity. In each \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) and \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) bin, the \(\Delta\phi\) distributions are divided by the \(\Delta\phi|_{\rm mix}\) distributions to correct the shapes for detector acceptance. Because the triggers in this analysis are multi-particle objects instead of single charged hadrons, the mixed

Figure 4.5: The single particle tracking efficiencies in _p+p_ (black dotted line) and Au+Au (black, red, and blue points for the 0-5%, 5-10%, and 10-20% centrality bins, respectively) are shown as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\). Details of the efficiency determination can be found in Appendix A.

event correction to the \(\Delta\phi\) shapes is not as large as in dihadron correlations analyses (where TPC sector boundaries cause deep dips in the pair acceptance). Some other two-particle correlation analyses also use mixed events to correct for the \(\Delta\eta\) shape of the correlation functions, shown in Fig. 4.6, but that is not done here. The correlation functions prior to background subtraction are shown in Appendix B, along with the magnitude of the mixed events correction.

#### The Associated Yields, Widths, and Mean \(p_{\rm T}\)

Once the efficiency-corrected \(\Delta\phi\) distributions have been calculated in each \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet,rec}\) and \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) bin, they are fit with a functional form in order to (statistically) separate the jet peaks from the heavy ion combinatoric background. The shapes of the \(\Delta\phi\) distributions are assumed to have the following functional form:

\[\frac{Y_{\rm NS}}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{\rm NS}^{2}}}e^{-(\Delta\phi )^{2}/2\sigma_{\rm NS}^{2}}+\frac{Y_{\rm AS}}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{\rm AS}^{2}}} e^{-(\Delta\phi-\pi)^{2}/2\sigma_{\rm AS}^{2}} \tag{4.4}\] \[+B\left(1+2v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\cos(2\Delta\phi)+2v _{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\cos(3\Delta\phi)\right)\]

which consists of a Gaussian peak at \(\Delta\phi=0\) representing the nearside jet peak, a Gaussian centered at \(\Delta\phi=\pi\) which represents the awayside jet peak, and a background term. In the fit function (although not shown in Eq. 4.4), the nearside peak is also present at \(\Delta\phi=\pm 2\pi,\pm 4\pi,\pm 6\pi\) and the awayside at \(\Delta\phi=-\pi,\pm 3\pi,\pm 5\pi,7\pi\), so that the tails of the Gaussians wrap around properly. In _p+p_ the background is a flat pedestal (no \(v_{2}\) or \(v_{3}\) terms), while in Au+Au it is assumed that the heavy-ion background is modulated by elliptic and "triangular" flow. If the associated hadrons and the trigger jets are each correlated with a common plane (the reaction plane or the participant planes), then the \(\Delta\phi\) distributions will have a background shape modulated by the cross-terms \(v_{n}^{\rm assoc}v_{n}^{\rm jet}\), which are shown in Eq. 4.4. The values of \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\) are discussed in detail in Section 4.6.

The raw \(\Delta\phi\) distributions are fit by the function shown in Equation 4.4 to obtain the nearside yield (\(Y_{\rm NS}\)), nearside width (\(\sigma_{\rm NS}\)), awayside yield (\(Y_{\rm AS}\)), and awayside width (\(\sigma_{\rm AS}\)). The mean transverse momentum of the charged hadrons (\(\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\rangle\)) is also calculated in three regions in \(\Delta\phi\)-space: on the nearside (\(-1<\Delta\phi<1\)), on the awayside (\(\pi-1.3<\Delta\phi<\pi+1.3\)), and in the background (\(\frac{\pi}{3}<\Delta\phi<\frac{2\pi}{3}\)). In some high-\(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) bins, \(\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm bkg}\rangle=0\) because there are no tracks in the region \(\frac{\pi}{3}<\Delta\phi<\frac{2\pi}{3}\); in these few cases \(\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm bkg}\rangle\) is set equal to \(\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm AS}\rangle\).

#### 4.5.3 Nearside Yield and \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) Correction in Au+Au

In Au+Au the nearside associated yield for \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}>2\) GeV/\(c\) is enhanced by background particles that are clustered into the jets. The mean \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) on the nearside (\(\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm NS}\rangle\)) is also affected by the inclusion of these particles. The values of \(Y_{\rm NS}(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc})\) and \(\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm NS}\rangle\) are corrected using the background level, \(B\), to estimate the number of background particles that fall within the jet cone. The estimated contribution from background fluctuations to the NS yield is given by:

\[\frac{1}{2}AB\left(1+2v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}+2v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{ 3}^{\rm jet}\right) \tag{4.5}\]

where \(A=\pi R^{2}=\pi\times 0.4^{2}\) is the nominal size of the jet cone. The factor of \(\frac{1}{2}\) accounts for the \(\Delta\eta\) acceptance. Since the trigger jet axis falls in the range \(|\eta|<0.6\) and the associated particles are restricted to \(|\eta|<1.0\), the \(\Delta\eta=\eta_{\rm assoc}-\eta_{\rm jet}\) acceptance has the shape shown in Figure 4.6. The integral of this curve is 2.0. Since \(B\) is a measure of \(dN/d\Delta\phi\) (integrated over \(\Delta\eta\)), it is necessary to divide by 2 to obtain a measure of \(d^{2}N/d\Delta\phi d\Delta\eta\). Then by multiplying by \(A\) (an area in \(\Delta\phi\)-\(\Delta\eta\) space), an estimate of the number of background particles within the jet cone can be obtained.

Therefore, in \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) bins above 2 GeV/\(c\), the fit function (Equation 4.4) is replaced by:

\[\left(Y_{\rm NS}+\frac{1}{2}AB\left(1+2v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet }+2v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\right)\right)\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{ \rm NS}^{2}}}e^{-(\Delta\phi)^{2}/2\sigma_{\rm NS}^{2}} \tag{4.6}\] \[+\frac{Y_{\rm AS}}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{\rm AS}^{2}}}e^{-(\Delta\phi -\pi)^{2}/2\sigma_{\rm AS}^{2}}+B\left(1+2v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet} \cos(2\Delta\phi)+2v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\cos(3\Delta\phi)\right)\]

The nearside mean \(p_{\rm T}\) is adjusted by the following formula:

\[\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm NS,new}\rangle Y_{\rm NS}= \langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm NS,old}\rangle\left(Y_{\rm NS}+\frac{1}{2} AB\left(1+2v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}+2v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet }\right)\right) \tag{4.7}\] \[-\frac{1}{2}AB\left(1+2v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}+2v_{3}^ {\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\right)\langle p_{\rm T}^{\rm bkg}\rangle\]

The nearside yield and \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) correction only has a large effect in the first \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) bin above \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}=2\) GeV/\(c\). In the higher \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) bins the background level is small and therefore so is the correction.

Background Subtraction

Jet \(v_{2}\) and \(v_{3}\) are _a priori_ unknown, so the approach in this analysis is to display the results under two extreme and opposite assumptions, while placing conservative uncertainties on the values of \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\). The two assumptions are the same as those used to place uncertainties on the jet energy scale (Section 4.4):

1. _HT trigger jets in Au+Au are unmodified compared to HT trigger jets in_ p+p. When fitting the \(\Delta\phi\) distributions with the functional form in (4.4) the nearside yields and widths in Au+Au are fixed to the values measured in _p+p_, \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) is fixed to a mean value and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\) is left as a free parameter. The mean \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}\) is estimated to be the average of \(v_{2}\{\rm FTPC\}(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc})\) and \(v_{2}\{4\}(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc})\), while \(v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) is estimated to be \(v_{2}\{\rm FTPC\}(6~{}{\rm GeV}/c)\), where \(v_{2}\{\rm FTPC\}(p_{\rm T})\) and \(v_{2}\{4\}(p_{\rm T})\) are obtained using a parameterization [62] from MB data [63]. Here, \(v_{2}\{\rm FTPC\}\) is estimated with respect to the event plane determined in the FTPCs and \(v_{2}\{4\}\) is determined using the 4-particle cumulant method [64]. The systematic uncertainties are determined by fixing \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) to maximum and minimum values while letting \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\) float to force the Au+Au nearside yields to match _p+p_. The lower and upper limits on \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}\) are estimated to be \(v_{2}\{4\}(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc})\) and \(v_{2}\{\rm FTPC\}(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc})\), respectively. The bounds on \(v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) are conservatively estimated to be 70% and 130% of \(v_{2}\{\rm FTPC\}(6~{}{\rm GeV}/c)\).
2. _HT trigger jets in Au+Au are maximally modified compared to HT trigger jets in_ p+p. The background assumption that produces the largest nearside yields and widths is \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}=0\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}=0\). (The possibility that \(v_{2}^{\rm jet}<0\) or \(v_{3}^{\rm jet}<0\) is not considered.)

The \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\) values that arise from each of these possibilities are shown in Figs. 4.7 and 4.8, respectively. As a cross-check, the \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\)values from a free fit are also shown, and it is good to see that they fall between the imposed minimum and maximum values. The \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\) values that result from the fits are reasonable compared to the data in [23, 65]. Note that as \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm assoc}\) increases, the background level \(B\) drops dramatically and therefore large uncertainties in the fitted values of \(v_{2}^{\rm assoc}v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm assoc}v_{3}^{\rm jet}\) are possible without having a significant impact on the final result. Examples of the background-subtracted \(\Delta\phi\) correlation function are shown in Fig. 4.9.

