The simulation was modified to include a background modulated by \(v_{2}\) and \(v_{3}\) and a jet correlated to \(\Psi_{\rm PP,2}\) and \(\Psi_{\rm PP,3}\) via \(v_{2}^{\rm jet}\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm jet}\). The underlying event \(v_{3}\) was chosen to equal the \(v_{2}\) measured by STAR in the 5-10% centrality bin [63]. The 2\({}^{\rm nd}\)- and 3\({}^{\rm rd}\)-order participant planes are each correlated with the jet axis, but are not explicitly correlated to each other. Figure 6.3 shows the results of a simulation in which 30 GeV jets are chosen to have \(v_{2}^{\rm jet}=0.1\) and \(v_{3}^{\rm jet}=0.3\). The new jet \(v_{n}\) method can accurately measure the jet \(v_{2}\) and \(v_{3}\) harmonics independently. The standard method estimation of jet \(v_{3}\) is less biased than the estimation of \(v_{2}^{\rm jet}\), because in the case of odd harmonics, the two jets in a dijet pair pull the event plane in opposite directions, and thus the bias is largely canceled.

